    <h1 style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">What Freelancers Need to Know About Credit Before They Apply for a Mortgage</h1>

    <p class="mt-8">If you've been freelancing for a few years and haven't paid close attention to your credit profile since you left salaried employment, this article is worth your time before you apply for a mortgage. Credit management looks different without employer-sponsored benefits, predictable payroll, or the financial infrastructure that comes with a traditional job, and a few patterns common among freelancers can create mortgage qualification problems that are entirely fixable with enough lead time.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">Why Freelance Financial Habits Create Specific Credit Risks</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">The credit patterns that hurt mortgage applicants most are the ones that develop gradually without anyone noticing. Variable income means some months are tight, and tight months sometimes produce late payments. Without a steady paycheck arriving on a predictable schedule, it's easier to miss a due date, carry a higher balance than planned, or let a card sit unused long enough that the issuer closes it. Any one of those events affects your credit score in ways that take months to recover from, and all of them are more common among freelancers than among salaried borrowers for reasons that have nothing to do with financial irresponsibility.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">Why Income Volatility Creates Credit Volatility</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">A freelancer with variable monthly income tends to carry variable credit card balances. In a strong month, balances get paid down. In a slow month, they climb. That fluctuation in utilization, the percentage of available credit being used at any given time, directly affects your credit score month to month. A lender who pulls your credit during a month when balances are high will see a lower score than one who pulls it after a strong billing cycle. For a salaried borrower, that variation is minimal. For a freelancer, it can be the difference between rate tiers.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">Beyond utilization, freelancers often carry fewer tradelines than salaried borrowers of similar age and income. Without employer-sponsored credit products or the financial product suite that often accompanies steady employment, the credit history can be thinner than the income would suggest. Thin credit isn't the same as bad credit, but it creates its own mortgage qualification challenges.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">How to Strengthen Your Credit Profile Before You Apply</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">The most effective credit improvement strategy for a freelancer is straightforward: pay down balances to below 10% of available credit across all cards and keep them there through the application and closing process. That single action, consistently maintained for two to three months before application, can produce a meaningful score increase for borrowers whose utilization has been running higher.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">Beyond utilization, check all three credit bureaus for errors before you apply. Freelancers who have moved, changed business structures, or operated under multiple business names over the years sometimes have reporting inconsistencies that suppress their scores without their knowledge. Disputing an error takes time. Start that process well before you need the score.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">If your credit history is thin rather than damaged, adding a credit product and managing it responsibly for 12 to 24 months before application builds the history lenders want to see. A secured card or a small installment loan used conservatively and paid on time every month adds depth to a thin file.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What to Do, In Order</h2>

    <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Pull all three credit reports at least 6 to 12 months before you plan to apply.</strong> Review each one for errors, closed accounts you didn't close, and unfamiliar activity. Give yourself time to dispute anything that needs correction.</li>
      <li><strong>Calculate your current credit utilization across all cards.</strong> Add up all your balances and divide by your total available credit. If that number is above 30%, paying it below 10% before application will improve your score.</li>
      <li><strong>Set up automatic minimum payments on every account.</strong> Variable income makes manual bill management unreliable. Automating minimums protects your payment history even in slow months.</li>
      <li><strong>Avoid closing old accounts even if you don't use them.</strong> The average age of your accounts and your total available credit both factor into your score. Closing an old card reduces both.</li>
      <li><strong>Do not open any new credit accounts in the 6 months before you apply for a mortgage.</strong> New accounts lower your average account age and create hard inquiries, both of which temporarily reduce your score.</li>
      <li><strong>Check your score monthly in the period leading up to your application.</strong> For a freelancer, score fluctuation tied to balance changes is normal. Knowing where you are each month lets you time your application to a strong credit window.</li>
    </ol>

